     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is twice the maximum labeled daily dose. I don't know of any indication that supports it, and it carries a real risk of serious harm, including severe GI toxicity and metformin-associated lactic acidosis. A normal renal function result doesn't remove that risk.\n\nYou can still get the order placed in a few ways:\n- **Use a standard dose.** I can enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day) or any lower dose right now.\n- **Talk to pharmacy or your attending.** If there's a clinical reason for going above the labeled maximum, they can review it and document the rationale. The order can then be entered with that documented.\n- **Check for a transcription slip.** 1700 mg may be the intended total daily dose, or 850 mg may have been meant per dose.\n\nTell me which dose you want and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
